.TH DMN-BLUEZ-DISCOVERY 1 "2025" "Dmn" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dmn-bluez-discovery \- list BlueZ devices or explicitly scan with a Bluetooth adapter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dmn-bluez-discovery
[
.B --discovery
]
[
.B --adapter
.I address-or-object-path
]
[
.B --help
]
.br
.B dmn-bluez-discovery
[
.B --adapter
.I address-or-object-path
]
.B --connect
.I device-address-or-object-path
[
.B --discovery
]
.br
.B dmn-bluez-discovery
[
.B --adapter
.I address-or-object-path
]
.B --disconnect
.I device-address-or-object-path
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dmn-bluez-discovery
prints BlueZ's known adapter and device inventory as CSV. Without
.BR --discovery ,
it performs a read-only inventory query and exits; it does not start a scan.
.PP
With
.BR --discovery ,
the program prints the initial inventory and then reports device additions,
changes, and removals while discovery is active. Press Ctrl-C or send SIGTERM
to stop the scan. The program stops only the discovery session it started.
.PP
Use
.B --connect
or
.B --disconnect
to explicitly act on one device selected by Bluetooth address or current
BlueZ object path. Connect can be combined with
.B --discovery
to scan for that selected target for up to 60 seconds; it never connects to
other scan results. A successful action is reported only after BlueZ's
.B Connected
state reflects the requested result.
.PP
This program reports devices known to BlueZ. It cannot guarantee that every
radio-visible device will be found; adapter capability, BlueZ policy, transport,
and scan conditions can affect results.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --discovery
Start an explicit scan after reading the initial inventory. The scan uses
BlueZ's default automatic transport and does not restrict results by UUID.
.TP
.BI "--adapter " address-or-object-path
Select a powered local adapter by its exact Bluetooth address or BlueZ object
path. If omitted during discovery, the program selects the adapter only when
exactly one powered adapter is available. When several are powered, specify
one explicitly. This option does not power an adapter on.
.TP
.BI "--connect " device-address-or-object-path
Request connection to exactly one device already present in BlueZ inventory.
If the address occurs on more than one adapter, use
.BR --adapter .
Combine with
.B --discovery
to wait up to 60 seconds for that specific target. The connection request and
state confirmation have a 30-second deadline. This does not call
.BR Device1.Pair ,
but BlueZ may start its normal system pairing or authorization flow if needed.
.TP
.BI "--disconnect " device-address-or-object-path
Request disconnection of exactly one known device. This does not remove its
pairing and may interrupt all active BlueZ profiles for that device.
Cannot be combined with
.BR --discovery .
.TP
.B --help
Print command usage and exit.
.SH OUTPUT
Standard output is RFC 4180 CSV, with a header row. Columns are
.BR action ,
.BR adapter_name ,
.BR adapter_address ,
.BR adapter_path ,
.BR adapter_powered ,
.BR device_address ,
.BR name ,
.BR alias ,
.BR reported_name ,
.BR paired ,
.BR connected ,
.BR services_resolved ,
.BR rssi ,
.BR device_path ,
and
.BR seen_during_discovery .
Unknown optional values are empty. Boolean values are
.B true
or
.BR false .
Fields containing commas, quotes, or line breaks are quoted and quotes are
escaped according to RFC 4180.
.PP
The initial inventory rows use the action
.BR inventory .
Live scan rows use
.BR added ,
.BR changed ,
or
.BR removed .
Explicit connection actions add a final
.B connected
or
.B disconnected
row only after the requested state is observed.
Diagnostics and errors are written to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print known adapters and devices without starting discovery:
.PP
.nf
dmn-bluez-discovery
.fi
.PP
Scan on one adapter by address:
.PP
.nf
dmn-bluez-discovery --discovery --adapter 'B8:F7:75:BF:F3:C3'
.fi
.PP
Scan by BlueZ object path:
.PP
.nf
dmn-bluez-discovery --discovery --adapter /org/bluez/hci0
.fi
.SH SAFETY
Inventory and scan-only modes are read-only with respect to devices. Explicit
connection actions call BlueZ
.B Device1.Connect
or
.BR Device1.Disconnect .
The program does not call
.B Device1.Pair
or register a pairing agent, though BlueZ may initiate system pairing or
authorization during a connection. Disconnect may interrupt other
applications' active profiles. These actions and scanning require permission
under local system D-Bus policy. The program uses the system bus and does not
fall back to the session bus.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Inventory completed, help was printed, or discovery stopped successfully.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid, unknown, duplicate, or incomplete command-line option.
.TP
.B 3
No powered Bluetooth adapter is available for discovery.
.TP
.B 4
BlueZ is unavailable or its service owner disappeared.
.TP
.B 5
Discovery could not start or could not be stopped cleanly.
.TP
.B 6
Adapter selection is ambiguous.
.TP
.B 7
The requested adapter does not match an available powered adapter.
.TP
.B 8
D-Bus connection, policy, inventory, or other runtime error.
.TP
.B 9
Selected device was not found or its address is ambiguous.
.TP
.B 10
BlueZ rejected the requested connection or disconnection action.
.TP
.B 11
The selected device did not reach the requested state before its deadline, or
was not discovered before the scan deadline.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Linux, a running BlueZ system service, and an available system D-Bus. Discovery
also requires an already-powered Bluetooth adapter and system-bus policy that
allows the requested operations. Build the program with
.B ENABLE_DBUS=ON
and
.BR ENABLE_BLUEZ=ON .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bluetoothctl (1),
.BR bluetoothd (8),
.BR dbus-daemon (1)
.PP
Project documentation: https://github.com/cheebinhoh/Dmn
